    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Dodge Charger Gets Special-Edition Package Celebrating 90 Years of Mopar\nAll-Electric MGU9 EV Arrives Now In Australia With Pricing on Par With the Toyota Land Cruiser Prado\n29 photos\nPhoto: MG Motor Australia\nMG Motor launched the all-new all-electric MGU9 EV back in September 2026 and offered all the juicy details aside from pricing – now the omission has been rectified.\nLast month, MG Motor Australia announced the main features and specifications of its \"highly anticipated\" MGU9 EV, the international version of the Maxus Terron 9 stemming from China’s SAIC Motor Corporation. MG is a well-known British automaker, established in 1942 as Morris Garages and in time became “one of the world’s most recognized automotive brands, famous for making stylish and affordable British sports cars.”\nHowever, the present is vastly different, and the future is all about making electric mobility more accessible across international markets, with the company now a subsidiary of the Chinese automaker SAIC since late 2007. Back to the Land Down Under, which has had an official MG presence since 2016, the company is launching quite a few new models this year, such as the all-new MG4 EV Urban , the all-electric MGS6 crossover SUV, or the limited-series MGU9 Black Edition pickup truck, among others.\nNow the MGU9 EV all-electric model is launching in Australia starting on October 29, 2026, with a full 3.5-ton towing capacity, “giving owners the ability to tow a caravan, boat or trailer while retaining the versatility expected of a full-size Ute.” The MGU9, by the way, is based on the Maxus Terron 9 BEV and diesel mid-size pickup truck produced by SAIC Maxus from 2024, and you can also find it as the LDV Terron 9 and the LDV eTerron 9 in Australia.\nTargeting the lifestyle EV pickup truck segment, the all-new MGU9 EV is coming with dual electric motors, offering a combined 325kW and 700 Nm of torque, or 436 hp and 516 lb-ft of torque, complete with all-wheel drive and a four-wheel Electronic Air Suspension along with a 102.2 kWh LFP battery pack. Range is up to 430 km on the WLTP cycle, which is about 267 miles on a single charge.\nMG also has a 236-liter frunk on the MGU9 EV , and the bed can take a standard pallet; there’s also vehicle-to-load (V2L) capability at 6.6 kW for use of power equipment through the external CCS2 outlet, and inside the cabin there are dual 12.3-inch screens, leather front seats, wireless Apple CarPlay and Android Auto, 15W wireless charging, and an eight-speaker JBL sound system, among other things. There was one final detail that remained unknown, though: pricing.\nNow MG has cleared the mystery and revealed the all-electric MGU9 EV will officially go on sale priced at AU$78,990 plus on-road costs. That’s almost $56k at the current exchange rate, but more importantly, in Australia it is the equivalent of a brand-new Toyota Land Cruiser Prado GX, which has a drive-away price of AU$78,869 with the 2.8-liter diesel turbo with 48-volt mild hybrid technology. That’s a nice lifestyle alternative, right?\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2027 MGU9 EV MGU9 EV MG Motor U9 EV mid-size pickup truck 2027MY pricing Australia\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT Alpina Prepping New 7er, Flagship Sedan Spied in a Premiere",
